    "body": "BLOCKED: PR #61 (branch `feat/issue-21`) implements the full issue — schema/DDL, the generic choke-point (idempotency + ifVersion), SchedulerStore (heartbeats, sweep, onItemEvent, timezone/DST), the rollup engine (config validation, mirror lifecycle checks, deterministic recompute, backfill), eager property-type migration, `packages/queue` (graphile-worker wrapper), the permission-manifest generator + owner_process drift check, the agent_runs delegation model with a `core.agentRun` stub, and the Semprec/System-settings seed. `pnpm build` and `pnpm test` (40 tests, run against a real embedded Postgres) both pass, and I did a self-review pass against `review-rules/` and fixed everything it found.\n\nI cannot get the PR to green, though: the required `review` check fails inside the private `code-review-bot` repo's own script, not in anything this PR touches:\n\n```\n/home/runner/work/semprec/semprec/.code-review-bot/scripts/run-review-github.sh: line 119: /usr/bin/jq: Argument list too long\n##[error]Process completed with exit code 126\n```\n\nThis reproduced identically on a retry (pushed an empty commit to retrigger — same line, same error, ~25s both times), so it isn't flaky. My working theory: this is the first PR since the repo went from scaffold-only to a real implementation, and this issue's diff (57 files, ~5k lines — the largest in the queue by design, since it's the foundation everything else stands on) is large enough to overflow whatever `jq` invocation `run-review-github.sh` builds at line 119 (most likely an `--argjson`/positional argument built from the full diff or file list rather than piped via stdin). I don't have access to `dvdtrsnk/code-review-bot` to confirm the exact line or fix it, and per the repo's own security review rules I shouldn't touch `.github/workflows/` to route around a required check even if I could.\n\nA human needs to either fix `run-review-github.sh` (pipe the large payload through stdin/a temp file instead of argv) or otherwise decide how to get this specific PR reviewed. I've left PR #61 open and pushed; issue #21 stays open per the work-issue runbook.",
